 respectively. The segment IDs are parse:...:0:126 and parse:...:0:139. That means segment P1 covers chars 0-126 of S1, P2 covers 0-139 of S2. If I request segment_refs P2, I get the whole new doc. But maybe I just need the missing section. The range read is more specific.\n\nHowever, I need to be careful: the instruction says \"Use short S/P/C/D references for reads.\" So I can do:\n```\nreads: {\n  ranges: [{source_ref: \"S2\", start_char: 93, end_char: 139}]\n}\n```\nBut wait, source_ref pattern is ^S[1-9][0-9]*$, so S2 is valid. start_char 93, end_char 139.\n\nAlternatively, I could finish with evidence_insufficient and note unresolved for the export section. But \"Request fixed reads for missing/cross-section evidence\" strongly suggests requesting a read. Since I have only partial evidence for the added section, requesting a read is the correct move. However, in the final answer to the user, I need to output the JSON. The user is simulating the agent. I think the appropriate response is action=read to get the missing 订单导出 text. But wait, the user might expect me to already know the content because they gave me the sections info. But the fixed_text_evidence is the only thing I can cite. I cannot cite T entries I don't have. I cannot make up quotes. The section title \"订单导出\" is in fixed_source_catalog but not in fixed_text_evidence. The rule candidate must cite T references from fixed_text_evidence. Since there's no T for that section, I cannot create a valid added rule candidate without the text. So I must request a read.\n\nActually, re-reading the prompt: \"In discovery, document_citations is a list of T references from fixed_text_evidence\". So any candidate I propose must have document_citations. If I don't have a T for the export section, I can't cite it. So I should request a read to get a T for it. But the read request doesn't directly request a T; it requests raw ranges. The server presumably will respond with more T evidence or the ranges themselves? The instruction says \"select supplied original text, never calculate/copy offsets, quotes, UUIDs or hashes. The server binds each T to its exact original text/position/hash and verifies it.\" This suggests T entries are provided in fixed_text_evidence. If I request a range read, the server might add a new T to fixed_text_evidence and then I can cite it. But in this single-turn response, I only have what's given. So if I choose action=finish, I can only propose rules for which I have T citations.\n\nGiven that, I have T1 and T2. T1 covers retention (old) and promo code (old). T2 covers retention (new). From T1 and T2, I can derive:\n- Modified rule: 已支付订单保留期从7天变更为9天 (from T1 to T2)\n- Removed rule: 优惠码提交规则删除 (from T1, present in old, absent in new based on T2 and section info)\n\nShould I request a read for the new export section? Yes, because it's cross-section evidence missing. The instruction says \"Request fixed reads for missing/cross-section evidence.\" So I'll return action=read requesting S2 range 93-139.\n\nBut the prompt says \"Return one impact-response-v3 JSON object using this response_schema.\" It doesn't say I must finish in one turn. I can choose read. Let's do that. However, the user might be evaluating whether I can finish with what I have. The \"discovery_instruction\" says \"候选引用实际读取的原文；不是需求评审。依据不足保留evidence_insufficient和unresolved。\" This means if evidence is insufficient, I keep evidence_insufficient. But \"Request fixed reads...\" suggests reading is preferred.\n\nIn many similar tasks, the expected behavior is: if you need more info, return action=read. Since there is a clear missing section, I should request a read. Let me formulate the read request.\n\n_read request:\n- ranges: source_ref S2, start_char 93, end_char 139 (the 订单导出 section).\n- maybe also segment_refs P2? But S2 range is enough.\n\nActually, I should also consider case_impacts. The protocol says: \"During case_impacts return BoundRuleCaseAssessment...\" But that's for the stage when rules are supplied. Here rules: [] and stage is discover_rules. So I don't need case_impacts yet. I just need to propose rule candidates in discovery? Wait, the response schema for candidates can be either AnchoredRuleCandidate or BoundRuleCaseAssessment. The coordination_context says stage is discover_rules. The discovery_instruction says propose rule change candidates from fixed original text and differences. So my candidates should be AnchoredRuleCandidate if I were finishing. But since I'm reading, candidates should be empty.\n\nWait, the protocol in the user message says: \"In discovery, document_citations is a list of T references from fixed_text_evidence; select supplied original text... For action=read supply reads with no final candidates/outcome; for finish provide unit_outcome/candidates/unresolved with no pending reads.\"\n\nSo for read:\n- protocol_version: \"impact-response-v3\"\n- action: \"read\"\n- summary: string\n- reads: BoundReadRequest\n- no candidates, no unit_outcome, no unresolved? Actually unresolved is part of the schema but the protocol says \"for finish provide unit_outcome/candidates/unresolved\". For read, we can omit them. But schema additionalProperties false. The required fields for read action are protocol_version, action, summary. reads is optional per schema? Let's check: properties has reads (BoundReadRequest), but required is [\"protocol_version\",\"action\",\"summary\"]. So reads is optional but expected for action=read.\n\nI'll provide:\n```json\n{\n  \"protocol_version\": \"impact-response-v3\",\n  \"action\": \"read\",\n  \"summary\": \"Request read for new 订单导出 section in S2 to assess added rule.\",\n  \"reads\": {\n    \"ranges\": [\n      {\n        \"source_ref\": \"S2\",\n        \"start_char\": 93,\n        \"end_char\": 139\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut is there any other missing evidence? The primary difference D1 shows deletion of promo code and replacement of retention period. T1 and T2 cover those. The new 管理鉴权 section: In S2, section-3 is 管理鉴权 (48-93). Old S1 section-3 was 优惠码 (48-83) and section-4 管理鉴权 (83-126). So in new doc, 管理鉴权 starts at 48 and ends at 93. Is its content changed? The difference didn't mention 管理鉴权 content changes, only retention and promo deletion. Since promo code removed, the doc shortened by 37 chars (83-48 = 35? Wait old promo section 48-83 is 35 chars? Actually start 48 end 83 length 35. New 管理鉴权 starts at 48, so it moved up. The text of 管理鉴权 might be identical, just shifted. We have no evidence of its content in T entries. But we don't need to cite it unless we think a rule changed there. The difference shows no operation touching 管理鉴权 text. So no rule candidate needed.\n\nThus only missing evidence is 订单导出.\n\nShould I also request read for case files? No, because in discovery we don't evaluate cases yet. Unless the instruction says to read cases for source sufficiency? The protocol says: \"没有规则仍判断本用例来源是否充分。\" This is under case_instruction for after rules are supplied. For discovery, maybe not needed. The discovery_instruction doesn't mention cases. So no case reads.\n\nOne more thing: Should I include segment_refs P2 instead of ranges? The ranges require start/end chars. I have the section info. It's valid. I'll use ranges.\n\nWait, but the instruction: \"Use short S/P/C/D references for reads.\" So S2 is a short reference. Good.\n\nNow, the schema for BoundRangeRead: source_ref pattern ^S[1-9][0-9]*$, start_char >=0, end_char >=0. required source_ref, start_char, end_char. Good.\n\nThe summary must be min length 1. Good.\n\nLet me double check if any other rules can be inferred from current evidence. The section change: old had 优惠码, new has 订单导出. The removal of 优惠码 is supported by T1 and D1. The addition of 订单导出 is visible in sections but not in T. Since the instruction says document_citations must be T references from fixed_text_evidence, and there is no T for 订单导出, I cannot create that candidate. I must either request read or